Leave the cursor's line for the shell to redraw

A shell's line editor redraws the line being edited after every
resize, on the assumption that the terminal left its rows alone: readline
moves up by the rows it last drew the line in and writes it again at the
new width. Reflow breaks that assumption. A typed command longer than a
row, narrowed one column at a time, came back with four partial copies
of itself in front of it, and widened, the redraw wrote over the line
above -- the first line of a two-line prompt. Measured against a real
bash in BeerSSH's suite.

Do not rewrap the logical line holding the cursor. Each of its rows
stays a row, cut at the new width when narrowing and joined to nothing
when widening, with its continuation marks kept, so a copy still reads
it as one line. Every other line is rewrapped as before.

The cost, chosen: output still being written on the cursor's line loses
what a narrowing cuts, as it would in a terminal that does not reflow.

t/69screen_reflow.test moves the cursor off the lines its two reflow
cases resize, and adds a case for the line that keeps its rows.

